{"reference":"2 Kings 14","book":"2 Kings","chapter":14,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions of Identification and Dating","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Verse 25 identifies the prophet as “Jonah son of Amittai.” Jonah 1:1 gives the same name and patronymic, so the two references may point to the same prophetic figure. That identification, however, does not establish that this eighth-century Jonah wrote the later third-person narrative in the book of Jonah; neither book makes that authorship claim.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 14:25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_014_b950cd90b452d060e34e48f3"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Jeroboam II’s reign overlaps with Uzziah’s, a pairing that can locate the setting of a superscription in the mid-eighth century BCE. That chronological setting does not, by itself, date every part of a text or determine when its final form was completed.","verse_refs":["2 Kings 14:23-29"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_kings_014_0b4a0ba3ff9f2b1ee7982770"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}